A seek stops CD audio, because on Win9x a seek IS the stop (doc 17 §5.4).

Reported by the user: MCI's "stop cd" does not stop the drive on Win98, and
XP's positions read oddly. Both were measured with the new
tools/cdaudio-guest-test.sh, which runs CDTEST.EXE on either family under
CDIMAGE_TRACE=1 and prints the audio commands the guest actually sent.

Win98's whole play / pause / stop session through MCI is **one PLAY AUDIO MSF
and two SEEKs**. Its mcicda sends no STOP PLAY/SCAN, no START STOP UNIT and no
PAUSE/RESUME at all: it seeks to where playback should end and then reports
"stopped" on its own authority. cmd_seek validated the LBA and returned OK, so
the drive played the rest of the disc out behind an MCI that believed it had
stopped it -- 11 MB of audio in the audiodev's wav from a four-second play.
MMC-3 has the seek end playback (the head goes elsewhere, so it cannot go on),
so that is what it does now, and the head is left where it was sent.

XP is the control and loses nothing by it: it brackets every play with
PAUSE, SEEK, PAUSE, PLAY -- so its seeks only ever happen while playback is
already stopped -- and it stops with START STOP UNIT, which was already
handled. Its 812 KB of wav for the same four seconds is what correct looks
like beside Win98's 11 MB.

Data reads are deliberately not treated the same way, although a real drive
does abandon playback for one: Win98's CDFS re-reads the volume descriptors
several times a second for the whole play (532 READ(10)s in that session), so
a drive that stopped audio on a read would never play a note under it.

The second half is the position. Once there was no audio status left to
return, the position replies fell back to the last sector *read*, which is a
data address that has nothing to do with the drive's head: XP, asked to play
track 3 after a stop, answered "track 01, 00:00:17" -- LBA 17 being its own
volume descriptor, read seconds earlier. The head now stays where playback
ended, and a seek puts it where it was sent, which is also what makes Win9x's
seek-as-stop report the position its own MCI shows.
diff --git a/hw/ide/atapi.c b/hw/ide/atapi.c
index 97adb48268..5e6da0003f 100644
--- a/hw/ide/atapi.c
+++ b/hw/ide/atapi.c
@@ -230,6 +230,16 @@ static void atapi_audio_stop(IDEState *s, uint8_t status)
 {
     if (s->atapi_audio_status == 0x11) {
         s->atapi_play_lba = atapi_audio_position(s);
+        /*
+         * The head stays where playback ended, and that is what the position
+         * replies report once there is no audio status left to return.
+         * Without this they fall back to the last sector *read*, so a guest
+         * that stops the drive and asks where it is gets told about whatever
+         * file it happened to read last: XP answered "track 01, 00:00:17"
+         * after being asked to play track 3, LBA 17 being its own volume
+         * descriptor (measured 2026-09-07).
+         */
+        s->atapi_last_lba = s->atapi_play_lba;
     }
     if (s->atapi_play_timer) {
         timer_del(s->atapi_play_timer);
@@ -249,7 +259,6 @@ static void atapi_audio_stop(IDEState *s, uint8_t status)
 static void atapi_audio_stop_play(IDEState *s)
 {
     atapi_audio_stop(s, 0x15);
-    s->atapi_play_lba = 0;
 }
 
 /*
@@ -1732,6 +1741,31 @@ static void cmd_seek(IDEState *s, uint8_t* buf)
         return;
     }
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_CDIMAGE
+    /*
+     * A seek ends any audio play in progress and leaves the head where it
+     * was sent (MMC-3: the head goes elsewhere, so playback cannot go on).
+     *
+     * This is not a detail — it is how Win9x stops a CD. Its mcicda sends
+     * neither STOP PLAY/SCAN nor START STOP UNIT: a whole play / pause /
+     * stop session through MCI is one PLAY AUDIO MSF and two SEEKs, and
+     * then it reports "stopped" on its own authority. A drive that treats a
+     * seek as advisory therefore plays the rest of the disc out behind an
+     * MCI that believes it is stopped. XP is the control and loses nothing:
+     * it seeks only while already paused (PAUSE, SEEK, PAUSE, PLAY) and
+     * stops with START STOP UNIT. Both measured 2026-09-07 with
+     * tools/cdaudio-guest-test.sh, which is also what guards this.
+     *
+     * Data reads are deliberately *not* treated this way: Win98's CDFS
+     * re-reads the volume descriptors several times a second throughout a
+     * play (532 READ(10)s in that session), so a drive that stopped audio
+     * on a read would never play a note under it.
+     */
+    if (atapi_disc(s)) {
+        atapi_audio_stop_play(s);
+        s->atapi_last_lba = lba;
+    }
+#endif
     ide_atapi_cmd_ok(s);
 }
 
